// PARAM: --analysis interval --context entry-state --context-graph expanded
// Same program as 03-procedures/precision/06-recursive_factorial_dead_branch_
// no_bottom_leak.vimp, which pins its four check verdicts; this fixture pins
// the per-context states those verdicts rest on, which no verdict surface can
// show. Entry-state keying gives one context per recursion depth, so
// factorial's single physical body renders as five clusters: n=[4,4], [3,3],
// [2,2], [1,1], and the empty-interval context the recursive call seeds once
// n's own range is exhausted. exit_factorial carries the exact ret=[24,24],
// [6,6], [2,2] and [1,1] of its own depth -- 24, 6, 2 and 1 are separated
// values here, not one widened summary interval, which is what
// known-imprecision/03-recursion.vimp shows the same source collapsing to
// when every activation shares one context key.
//
// The n<2 base-case branch is the dead-branch half: pp2 renders "unreachable"
// in every n>=2 cluster, so its own "return 1" edge contributes nothing to
// that depth's exit join, while pp2 in the n=[1,1] cluster is live and returns
// [1,1]. The same split shows on the two checks: 0<n and 0<r render "[dead]"
// in the exhausted-range cluster (and 0<r also in n=[1,1], which never makes
// the recursive call) and decide normally everywhere else, so both source-level
// verdicts come from the live activations alone.
// EXPECT-GRAPH-BEGIN
// clusters:
//   cluster_ctx_0:
//     main_pp10_ctx0
//     main_pp11_ctx0
//     main_result_main_ctx0
//     main_entry_main_ctx0
//     main_pp7_ctx0
//     main_pp8_ctx0
//     main_pp9_ctx0
//   cluster_ctx_1:
//     factorial_pp1_ctx1
